bibit-runtime-plugin (0.1.50) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Move the security permission settings onto their own security page. The
    page now has six tabs (agents, capabilities, permission settings,
    syscalls, DLP and audit) so the scopes are no longer buried under the
    capability table.
  * Replace the flat read and write path lists with include + exclude pairs,
    and drop the custom JSON group. An excluded read path becomes invisible
    while an excluded write path stays readable, which is what the Runtime
    compiles into its mount boundaries.
  * Show the execution mode read-only. host or sandbox follows from the
    declared settings, so the page reports the derived result instead of
    offering a selector.
  * Remove the syscall denylist baseline from the syscall page: nothing is
    mandatory, entries start unchecked and the page says so.
  * Drop every security control from the Expert editor. Capability policies,
    the JSON policy panel, the network scope, the personal-data policy and
    the host/sandbox selector now live only in the security control center,
    and the editor no longer sends permissions or a requested execution mode.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 20:50:00 +0800

bibit-runtime-plugin (0.1.49) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Default every tool policy to `allow`. The expert editor and the security
    console both fell back to `ask` when a tool had no declared policy, which
    contradicts the shipped default. The fallback is now `allow` everywhere it
    is derived, while an explicitly declared policy is left untouched.
  * Split the syscall denylist into its own security page. It is a kernel
    seccomp table, not a filesystem or network scope, so the security page now
    has five tabs (agents, capabilities, syscalls, DLP and audit). The new page
    carries the architecture selector, the mandatory-versus-optional split and
    the interception audit that used to sit inside the capability page.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:25:00 +0800

bibit-runtime-plugin (0.1.48) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Keep watching an MCP server after it is installed or removed. The Runtime
    answers an install from the Control catalog while the Worker may still be
    rebuilding its Generation, so the page used to show `not_loaded` right
    after a successful install and then stop looking. The MCP page now polls
    for a bounded settling window and re-arms that poll after every mutation.
  * Correct the `not_loaded` explanation: the Runtime prepares every installed
    server once a Worker is ready, whether or not an Expert selects it.
  * Track the Runtime security page change: capability management is now one
    dimension, the tool, with three states (allow, ask, deny). The capability
    page lists a policy per tool plus the five security permission groups, the
    DLP page uses the same three states plus `mask`, and the security page
    keeps four tabs (agents, capabilities, DLP and audit). The tool catalog is
    scoped by framework, so an Expert bound to the Runtime sees the Runtime
    tools and one bound to hermes sees the hermes tools.

